1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,714
You send a photo to a friend. The network
carries its

2
00:00:04,714 --> 00:00:09,429
data in smaller pieces instead of one enormous
object. Watch one

3
00:00:09,429 --> 00:00:14,143
piece leave your computer and arrive at
theirs. In this video

4
00:00:14,143 --> 00:00:18,857
we will follow TCP, a set of rules computers
can use

5
00:00:18,857 --> 00:00:23,571
to deliver data reliably. A piece of TCP data
is called

6
00:00:23,571 --> 00:00:28,286
a segment. Our question: how many pieces
should we send before

7
00:00:28,286 --> 00:00:33,000
waiting to hear back?

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,679
Sending something is not the same as knowing
it arrived. So

9
00:00:37,679 --> 00:00:42,358
the receiving computer sends a small reply
meaning, I've received data

10
00:00:42,358 --> 00:00:47,038
up to this point. That reply is an
acknowledgment, shortened to

11
00:00:47,038 --> 00:00:51,717
ACK. Watch blue data go right and a green
reply return

12
00:00:51,717 --> 00:00:56,396
left. We will draw one reply for each piece to
keep

13
00:00:56,396 --> 00:01:01,075
the idea clear. Actual TCP can acknowledge
several pieces together. The

14
00:01:01,075 --> 00:01:05,754
returning replies give the sender evidence of
progress instead of making

15
00:01:05,754 --> 00:01:10,433
it guess blindly.

16
00:01:10,433 --> 00:01:15,058
Let's begin with an allowance of four pieces.
The four empty

17
00:01:15,058 --> 00:01:19,683
boxes are spaces for data we have sent but
haven't yet

18
00:01:19,683 --> 00:01:24,308
had confirmed. This allowance is called the
congestion window, often written

19
00:01:24,308 --> 00:01:28,933
cwnd. Here, window means a limit, not
something on your screen.

20
00:01:28,933 --> 00:01:33,558
We chose four for this demonstration; real
starting values can differ.

21
00:01:33,558 --> 00:01:38,183
Also assume the receiver has plenty of room.
That lets us

22
00:01:38,183 --> 00:01:42,808
focus on how the sender grows its allowance
using feedback from

23
00:01:42,808 --> 00:01:47,433
the journey.

24
00:01:47,433 --> 00:01:52,462
Send piece one. Fill the first box. Send piece
two, then

25
00:01:52,462 --> 00:01:57,490
three, then four. All four spaces are now
occupied. We call

26
00:01:57,490 --> 00:02:02,519
this data outstanding: sent, but not yet
confirmed by a reply.

27
00:02:02,519 --> 00:02:07,548
The sender cannot keep adding pieces beyond
its allowance. It waits

28
00:02:07,548 --> 00:02:12,576
for progress. That waiting isn't wasted
caution. The sender doesn't yet

29
00:02:12,576 --> 00:02:17,605
know how much traffic the path can handle. The
blue boxes

30
00:02:17,605 --> 00:02:22,633
show exactly what it is waiting to hear about.

31
00:02:22,633 --> 00:02:27,312
Now the first green reply arrives. Two things
happen. First, one

32
00:02:27,312 --> 00:02:31,992
piece is confirmed, so it no longer occupies a
space. Second,

33
00:02:31,992 --> 00:02:36,671
during our version of slow start, this reply
increases the allowance

34
00:02:36,671 --> 00:02:41,350
by one piece. Our limit rises from four to
five, with

35
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:46,029
three old pieces still unconfirmed. Five
spaces minus three occupied spaces

36
00:02:46,029 --> 00:02:50,708
leaves room for two new pieces. Watch both
spaces open. A

37
00:02:50,708 --> 00:02:55,388
reply both clears old work and gives us
permission to try

38
00:02:55,388 --> 00:03:00,067
a little more.

39
00:03:00,067 --> 00:03:04,800
Let the other replies come back too. Under our
one-reply-per-piece assumption,

40
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:09,533
each adds one space. Starting with four
spaces, four replies increase

41
00:03:09,533 --> 00:03:14,267
the limit to eight. That's the surprise: we
added only one

42
00:03:14,267 --> 00:03:19,000
at a time, yet a complete round doubled the
allowance. We

43
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,733
haven't measured a perfect network speed.
We've learned that this batch

44
00:03:23,733 --> 00:03:28,467
got through and cautiously allowed more data.
The rule is called

45
00:03:28,467 --> 00:03:33,200
slow start because it begins with limited
data, even though its

46
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:37,933
growth soon becomes rapid.

47
00:03:37,933 --> 00:03:42,500
Real replies don't have to arrive as one tidy
pile at

48
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:47,067
the end of a round. Watch them return
separately. Each reply

49
00:03:47,067 --> 00:03:51,633
can make room for more sending immediately.
This gives the data

50
00:03:51,633 --> 00:03:56,200
a rhythm: progress comes back, more data goes
out. People call

51
00:03:56,200 --> 00:04:00,767
this the acknowledgment clock. It isn't a
separate clock inside the

52
00:04:00,767 --> 00:04:05,333
cable. It describes how returning feedback
helps pace the sender. Our

53
00:04:05,333 --> 00:04:09,900
arrows are deliberately slow so you can follow
that cause and

54
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:14,467
effect.

55
00:04:14,467 --> 00:04:19,562
Now repeat the experiment with eight pieces.
Eight replies can add

56
00:04:19,562 --> 00:04:24,657
eight more spaces, making sixteen. Sixteen can
then become thirty-two. Look

57
00:04:24,657 --> 00:04:29,752
at the dots rising on the graph: four, eight,
sixteen, thirty-two.

58
00:04:29,752 --> 00:04:34,848
Each step represents a complete ideal growth
round, not a fixed

59
00:04:34,848 --> 00:04:39,943
second. Twice as many pieces can produce twice
as many replies,

60
00:04:39,943 --> 00:04:45,038
which can create twice as much room. That's
why adding one

61
00:04:45,038 --> 00:04:50,133
per reply produces such a fast-growing pattern
over several rounds.

62
00:04:50,133 --> 00:04:55,290
We can write what the picture already showed.
Begin with four,

63
00:04:55,290 --> 00:05:00,448
then multiply by two once for every completed
round. After k

64
00:05:00,448 --> 00:05:05,605
rounds, the allowance is four times two to the
power k.

65
00:05:05,605 --> 00:05:10,762
That power just means repeated multiplication:
after two rounds, four times

66
00:05:10,762 --> 00:05:15,919
two times two equals sixteen. At zero rounds
we still have

67
00:05:15,919 --> 00:05:21,076
four. You don't need the formula to discover
the pattern; it

68
00:05:21,076 --> 00:05:26,233
is a short way to describe the doubling we
just watched.

69
00:05:26,233 --> 00:05:30,858
Does an allowance of sixteen pieces tell us
the download speed?

70
00:05:30,858 --> 00:05:35,483
Not by itself. We also need the time for data
to

71
00:05:35,483 --> 00:05:40,108
go out and feedback to return. That's the
round-trip time, or

72
00:05:40,108 --> 00:05:44,733
RTT. Suppose each piece carries one thousand
bytes and a round

73
00:05:44,733 --> 00:05:49,358
trip takes one tenth of a second. Sixteen
thousand bytes divided

74
00:05:49,358 --> 00:05:53,983
by that time gives one hundred sixty thousand
bytes per second.

75
00:05:53,983 --> 00:05:58,608
Eight bits make a byte, so that's about 1.28
million bits

76
00:05:58,608 --> 00:06:03,233
per second.

77
00:06:03,233 --> 00:06:07,858
Keep those same sixteen spaces, but make the
round trip twice

78
00:06:07,858 --> 00:06:12,483
as long: two tenths of a second. Reusing the
allowance now

79
00:06:12,483 --> 00:06:17,108
takes longer, so our estimated rate halves to
0.64 million bits

80
00:06:17,108 --> 00:06:21,733
per second. Nothing shrank the boxes. Feedback
simply returned more slowly.

81
00:06:21,733 --> 00:06:26,358
This estimate assumes the window is the main
limit and data

82
00:06:26,358 --> 00:06:30,983
keeps flowing. It helps explain why a window
size and a

83
00:06:30,983 --> 00:06:35,608
rate are different things: one counts how
much; the other also

84
00:06:35,608 --> 00:06:40,233
includes time.

85
00:06:40,233 --> 00:06:44,967
Can we keep doubling forever? Watch the narrow
link in the

86
00:06:44,967 --> 00:06:49,700
middle. A link is one connection along the
route. This one

87
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:54,433
can pass data more slowly than the sender now
brings it.

88
00:06:54,433 --> 00:06:59,167
Pieces start waiting, just like people
arriving faster than a door

89
00:06:59,167 --> 00:07:03,900
lets them through. This limiting connection is
called a bottleneck. More

90
00:07:03,900 --> 00:07:08,633
data in the waiting line does not make that
connection faster.

91
00:07:08,633 --> 00:07:13,367
Eventually the sender needs to change how it
grows, rather than

92
00:07:13,367 --> 00:07:18,100
doubling without a boundary.

93
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:22,779
TCP uses a boundary called the slow-start
threshold. The short label

94
00:07:22,779 --> 00:07:27,458
on the diagram is ssthresh. Below that
boundary, slow-start growth can

95
00:07:27,458 --> 00:07:32,138
continue. At the boundary, the sender moves to
another growth phase.

96
00:07:32,138 --> 00:07:36,817
In our picture we chose thirty-two so you can
see where

97
00:07:36,817 --> 00:07:41,496
the steep curve stops. This is a teaching
value, not a

98
00:07:41,496 --> 00:07:46,175
number shared by every connection. Loss and
other feedback can also

99
00:07:46,175 --> 00:07:50,854
change the behavior. Today we're following
this opening growth idea, not

100
00:07:50,854 --> 00:07:55,533
every TCP rule.

101
00:07:55,533 --> 00:08:00,629
Let's change just one starting condition.
Begin with eight spaces instead

102
00:08:00,629 --> 00:08:05,724
of four. Our ideal sequence is now eight,
sixteen, thirty-two. We

103
00:08:05,724 --> 00:08:10,819
reach thirty-two one round earlier. A bigger
starting allowance can save

104
00:08:10,819 --> 00:08:15,914
early waiting, but it also places more data
onto a path

105
00:08:15,914 --> 00:08:21,010
whose capacity we may not know. The two
colored curves make

106
00:08:21,010 --> 00:08:26,105
that tradeoff visible. There isn't one magic
starting number that makes

107
00:08:26,105 --> 00:08:31,200
every network behave the same way under every
possible load.

108
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:35,825
If you record a real download, don't expect
every curve to

109
00:08:35,825 --> 00:08:40,450
match our neat staircase. Perhaps the app has
no more data

110
00:08:40,450 --> 00:08:45,075
ready. Perhaps replies confirm several pieces
at once. Perhaps the receiver

111
00:08:45,075 --> 00:08:49,700
has limited space, or data gets lost along the
way. Each

112
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:54,325
changes the picture. Our demonstration
deliberately kept these distractions out so

113
00:08:54,325 --> 00:08:58,950
one cause was visible: replies can grow the
sending allowance. Now

114
00:08:58,950 --> 00:09:03,575
you can spot the idea without treating our
drawing as a

115
00:09:03,575 --> 00:09:08,200
universal stopwatch.

116
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:12,767
Return to that first photo. We began with
small pieces, learned

117
00:09:12,767 --> 00:09:17,333
what a reply tells us, and used four spaces to
track

118
00:09:17,333 --> 00:09:21,900
unconfirmed data. Each successful reply both
released space and helped grow

119
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:26,467
the allowance. Over ideal rounds, four became
eight, then sixteen. The

120
00:09:26,467 --> 00:09:31,033
sender didn't need a sign announcing the
perfect speed. It began

121
00:09:31,033 --> 00:09:35,600
with a limit and learned from feedback. That
is the central

122
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:40,167
idea of TCP slow start: start small, listen to
progress, and

123
00:09:40,167 --> 00:09:44,733
grow.
